⟦200⟧importance in connexion with the differential equations of physics. The two equations
{{block center|<math>\frac{\partial u}{\partial t} = k\nabla^2 u,\ \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial t^2} = k^2\nabla^2 u,</math>}}
are reducible by means of the substitutions <math>u = e^{-kt}v</math>, <math>u = e^{\iota kt}v</math> to the form <math>\nabla^2 v + v = 0</math>. If we suppose <math>v</math> to be a function of <math>r</math> only, this last differential equation takes the form
{{block center|<math>\frac{d^2(vr)}{dr^2} + vr = 0,</math>}}
so that <math>v</math> has the values
{{block center|<math>\sin r/r,\ \cos r/r;</math>}}
in order to obtain more general solutions of the equation <math>\nabla^2 v + v = 0</math>, we may operate on
{{block center|<math>\sin r/r,\ \cos r/r</math>}}
with the operator
{{block center|<math>\text{Y}_n\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial x}, \frac{\partial}{\partial y}, \frac{\partial}{\partial z}\right),</math>}}
where <math>\text{Y}_n\ (x,\ y,\ z)</math> is any spherical solid harmonic of degree <math>n</math>. The result of the operation may be at once obtained by taking <math>\text{Y}_n\ (x,\ y,\ z)</math> for <math>f_n(x,\ y,\ z)</math> in the theorem (7′), we thus find as solutions, of <math>\nabla^2 v + v = 0</math>, the expressions
{{block center|<math>\text{Y}_n(x,\ y,\ z)\frac{d^n}{d(r^2)^n}\frac{\sin r}{r},\ \text{Y}_n(x,\ y,\ z)\frac{d^n}{d(r^2)^n}\frac{\cos r}{r}.</math>}}

⟦1100⟧By recurring to the definition of the function <math>\text{J}_m(r)</math>, we see that
{{block center|<math>\text{J}_{\frac{1}{2}}(r) = \sqrt{\frac{2\rho}{\pi}}\left\{1 - \frac{r^2}{2\cdot3} + \frac{r^4}{2\cdot3\cdot4\cdot5} - \dots\right\} = \sqrt{\frac{2}{\pi}}\frac{\sin r}{\sqrt{r}};</math>}}
thus
{{block center|<math>r^{-\frac{1}{2}}\text{J}_{\frac{1}{2}}(r) = \sqrt{\frac{2}{\pi}}\frac{\sin r}{r}.</math>}}
Using the relation between Bessel's functions whose orders differ by an integer, we have
{{block center|<math>\text{J}_{n+\frac{1}{2}}(r) = (-2)^n r^{n+\frac{1}{2}}\frac{d^n}{d(r^2)^n}\frac{\text{J}_{\frac{1}{2}}(r)}{\sqrt{r}} = (-2)^n\sqrt{\frac{2}{\pi}}r^{n+\frac{1}{2}}\frac{d^n}{d(r^2)^n}\frac{\sin r}{r}.</math>}}
It may be shown at once that
{{block center|<math>r^{n+\frac{1}{2}}\frac{d^n}{d(r^2)^n}\frac{\cos r}{r}</math>}}
is a second solution of Bessel's equation of order <math>n+\tfrac{1}{2}</math>; thus the differential equation <math>\nabla^2 v + v = 0</math> is satisfied by the expression
{{block center|<math>\text{Y}_n(x,\ y,\ z)\frac{\text{J}_{n+\frac{1}{2}}(r)}{r^{n+\frac{1}{2}}},</math>}}
and by the corresponding expression with a second solution of Bessel's equation instead of <math>\text{J}_{n+\frac{1}{2}}(r)</math>; if <math>\text{S}_n(\mu,\ \phi)</math> denotes a surface harmonic of degree <math>n</math>, the expression
{{block center|<math>\text{S}_n(\mu,\ \phi)\frac{1}{\sqrt{r}}\text{J}_{n+\frac{1}{2}}(r)</math>}}
is a solution of the equation <math>\nabla^2 v + v = 0</math>.

⟦2000⟧The Bessel's functions of degree half an odd integer are the only ones which are expressible in a closed form involving no transcendental functions other than circular functions. It will be observed that in this case the semi-convergent series for <math>\text{J}_m</math> becomes a finite one as the expressions <math>\text{P}</math>, <math>\text{Q}</math> then break off after a finite number of terms.

⟦2200⟧28. ''The Zeros of Bessel's Functions''.—The determination of the position of the zeros of the Bessel's functions, and the values of the argument at which they occur, have been investigated by Hurwitz (''Math. Ann.'' vol. xxxiii.), and more completely by H. M. Macdonald (''Proc. Lond. Math. Soc.'' vols. xxix., xxx.). It has been shown that the zeros of <math>\text{J}_n(z)/z^n</math> are all real and associated with the singular point at infinity when <math>n</math> is real and <math>> -1</math>, and that all the real zeros of <math>\text{J}_n(z)/z^n</math> when <math>n</math> is real and <math>< -1</math>, and not an integer, are associated with the essential singularity at infinity. When <math>n</math> is a negative integer <math>-m</math>, <math>\text{J}_n(z)/z^n</math> has, in addition, <math>2m</math> real zeros coincident at the origin. When <math>n = -m - v</math>, <math>m</math> being a positive integer, and <math>1 > v > 0</math>, <math>\text{J}_n(z)/z^n</math> has a finite number <math>2m</math> of zeros which are not associated with the essential singularity. If <math>n</math> is real, and starts with any positive value, the zeros nearest the origin approach it as <math>n</math> diminishes, two of them reaching it when <math>n = -1</math>, and two more reach it whenever <math>n</math> passes through a negative integral value; these zeros then become complex for values of <math>n</math> not integral. The zeros of <math>\text{J}_n(z)/z^n</math> are separated by those of <math>\text{J}_{n+1}(z)/z^n</math>, one zero of the latter, and one only, lies between two consecutive zeros of <math>\text{J}_n(z)/z^n</math>. When <math>n</math> is real and <math>> -1</math>, all the zeros of <math>\text{J}_n(z)/z^n</math> are given by a formula due to Stokes; the <math>m^{th}</math> positive zero in order of magnitude is given by
{{block center|<math>a = \frac{4n^2-1}{8\alpha} - \frac{4(4n^2-1)(28n^2-31)}{3\cdot(8\alpha)^3} - \&\text{c}.,</math>}}
where <math>\alpha = \tfrac{1}{4}\pi(2n+4m-1)</math>. It has been shown by Macdonald

