⟦200⟧that the function <math>\text{K}_n(z)</math> has no real zeros unless <math>n=2k+\tfrac{3}{2}</math> where <math>k</math> is an integer, when it has one real negative zero; and that <math>\text{K}_n(z)</math> has no purely imaginary zeros, and no zero whose real part is positive, other than those at infinity. When <math>1>n>0</math>, <math>\text{K}_n(z)</math> has no zeros other than those at infinity, when <math>2>n>1</math>, it has one zero whose real part is negative, and when <math>m+1>n>m</math> where <math>m</math> is an integer, there are <math>m</math> zeros whose real parts are negative. When <math>n</math> is an integer, <math>\text{K}_n(z)</math> has <math>n</math> zeros with negative real parts.

⟦400⟧29. ''Spheroidal Harmonics''.—For potential problems in which the boundary is an ellipsoid of revolution, the co-ordinates to be used are <math>r</math>, <math>\theta</math>, <math>\phi</math> where in the case of a prolate spheroid
{{block center|<math>x=c\sqrt{r^2-1}\sin\theta\cos\phi,\ y=c\sqrt{r^2-1}\sin\theta\sin\phi,\ z=cr\cos\theta,</math>}}
the surfaces <math>r=r_0</math>, <math>\theta=\theta_0</math>, <math>\phi=\phi_0</math> are confocal prolate spheroids, confocal hyperboloids of revolution, and planes passing through the axis of revolution. We may suppose <math>r</math> to range from <math>1</math> to <math>\infty</math>, <math>\theta</math> from <math>0</math> to <math>\pi</math>, and <math>\phi</math> from <math>0</math> to <math>2\pi</math>, every point in space has then unique co-ordinates <math>r</math>, <math>\theta</math>, <math>\phi</math>.

⟦700⟧For oblate spheroids, the corresponding co-ordinates are <math>r</math>, <math>\theta</math>, <math>\phi</math> given by
{{block center|<math>x=c\sqrt{r^2+1}\sin\theta\cos\phi,\ y=c\sqrt{r^2+1}\sin\theta\sin\phi,\ z=cr\cos\theta,</math>}}
where
{{block center|<math>0\leqq r\leqq\infty,\ 0\leqq\theta\leqq\pi,\ 0\leqq\phi\leqq 2\pi;</math>}}
these may be obtained from those for the prolate spheroid by changing <math>c</math> into <math>-\iota c</math>, and <math>r</math> into <math>\iota r</math>.

⟦1100⟧Taking the case of the prolate spheroid, Laplace's equation becomes
{{block center|<math>\frac{\partial}{\partial r}\left\{(r^2-1)\frac{\partial\text{V}}{\partial r}\right\}+\frac{1}{\sin\theta}\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}\left(\sin\theta\frac{\partial\text{V}}{\partial\theta}\right)+\frac{r^2-\cos^2\theta}{(r^2-1)\sin^2\theta}\frac{\partial^2\text{V}}{\partial\phi^2}=0,</math>}}
and it will be found that the normal solutions are
{{block center|<math>\left.\begin{matrix}\text{P}^m_n(r)\\ \text{Q}^m_n(r)\end{matrix}\right\}\left.\begin{matrix}\text{P}^m_n(\cos\theta)\\ \text{Q}^m_n(\cos\theta)\end{matrix}\right\}\begin{matrix}\cos\\ \sin\end{matrix}m\phi.</math>}}
For the space inside a bounding spheroid the appropriate normal forms are <math>\text{P}^m_n(r)\text{P}^m_n(\cos\theta)\begin{matrix}\cos\\ \sin\end{matrix}m\phi</math>, where <math>n</math>, <math>m</math> are positive integers, and for the external space
{{block center|<math>\text{Q}^m_n(r)\text{P}^m_n(\cos\theta)\begin{matrix}\cos\\ \sin\end{matrix}m\phi.</math>}}
For the case of an oblate spheroid, <math>\text{P}^m_n(\iota r)</math>, <math>\text{Q}^m_n(\iota r)</math>, take the place of <math>\text{P}^m_n(r)</math>, <math>\text{Q}^m_n(r)</math>.

⟦1800⟧30. ''Toroidal Functions''.—For potential problems connected with the anchor-ring, the following co-ordinates are appropriate: If A, B are points at the extremities of a diameter of a fixed circle, and P is any point in the plane PAB which is perpendicular to the plane of the fixed circle, let <math>\text{P}=\log(\text{AP}/\text{BP})</math>, <math>\theta=\angle\text{APB}</math>, and let <math>\phi</math> be the angle the plane APB makes with a fixed plane through the axis of the circle. Let <math>\theta</math> be restricted to lie between <math>-\pi</math> and <math>\pi</math>, a discontinuity in its value arising as we pass through the circle, so that within the circumference <math>\theta</math> is <math>\pi</math> on the upper side of the circle, and <math>-\pi</math> on the lower side; <math>\theta</math> is zero in the plane of the circle outside the circumference; <math>\rho</math> may have any value between <math>-\infty</math> and <math>\infty</math>, and <math>\phi</math> any value between <math>0</math> and <math>2\pi</math>. The position of a point is then uniquely represented by the co-ordinates <math>\rho</math>, <math>\theta</math>, <math>\phi</math>, which are the parameters of a system of tores with the fixed circle as limiting circle, a system of bowls with the fixed circle as common rim, and a system of planes through the axis of the tores. If <math>x</math>, <math>y</math>, <math>z</math> are the co-ordinates of a point referred to axes, two of which <math>x</math>, <math>y</math> are in the plane of the circle and the third along its axis, we find that
{{block center|<math>x=\frac{a\sinh\rho}{\cosh\rho-\cos\theta}\cos\phi,\ y=\frac{a\sinh\rho}{\cosh\rho-\cos\theta}\sin\phi,\ z=\frac{a\sin\theta}{\cosh\rho-\cos\theta},</math>}}
where <math>a</math> is the radius of the fixed circle.

⟦2500⟧Laplace's equation reduces to
{{block center|<math>\frac{\partial}{\partial\rho}\left\{\frac{\sinh\rho}{\text{P}^2}\frac{\partial\text{V}}{\partial\rho}\right\}+\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}\left\{\frac{\sinh\rho}{\text{P}^2}\frac{\partial\text{V}}{\partial\theta}\right\}+\frac{1}{\text{P}^2\sinh\rho}\frac{\partial^2\text{V}}{\partial\phi^2}=0,</math>}}
when P denotes <math>\sqrt{(\cosh\rho-\cos\theta)}</math>. It can be shown that this equation is satisfied by
{{block center|<math>\sqrt{(\cosh\rho-\cos\theta)}\begin{matrix}\text{P}^m_{n-\frac{1}{2}}(\cosh\rho)\\ \text{Q}^m_{n-\frac{1}{2}}(\cosh\rho)\end{matrix}\begin{matrix}\cos\\ \sin\end{matrix}n\theta\begin{matrix}\cos\\ \sin\end{matrix}m\phi,</math>}}
the functions <math>\text{P}^m_{n-\frac{1}{2}}(\cosh\rho)</math>, <math>\text{Q}^m_{n-\frac{1}{2}}(\cosh\rho)</math> required for the potential problems, are associated Legendre's functions of degree <math>n-\tfrac{1}{2}</math>, half an odd integer, of integral order <math>m</math>, and of argument real and greater than unity; these are known as toroidal functions. For the space external to a boundary tore the function <math>\text{Q}^m_{n-\frac{1}{2}}(\cosh\rho)</math> must be used, and for the internal space <math>\text{P}^m_{n-\frac{1}{2}}(\cosh\rho)</math>.
